Lab 4 Answers
=============

1. Without a volume, MLflow writes to the container's writable layer. The data
   remains while that container exists, but removing the container deletes it.
   A new container made from the image starts with an empty MLflow database.

2. A named volume is managed by Docker, is portable across Compose runs, and
   avoids host path and permission differences. A bind mount would also persist
   the files and can be convenient for inspecting them during development, but
   it couples the setup to a particular host directory.

3. Docker Compose creates a private network and embedded DNS records for its
   services. On that network, `mlflow` resolves to the MLflow container. A
   standalone Lab 3 container was not on such a network, so that name did not
   exist and it needed a special route to the host instead.

4. The environment variable makes the same frontend image usable in different
   environments. In Compose it uses `http://inference:8000`; when run on its own
   it can use the default localhost URL or be pointed at another API.

5. Only other containers need direct access to inference. The frontend reaches
   `http://inference:8000` over the private Compose network, so no host port is
   required. MLflow and Streamlit publish ports because a human opens their UIs.

6. `depends_on` only starts MLflow first. If inference tries to load the model
   before MLflow is ready, its startup raises an exception and its container
   exits. It can then be restarted after MLflow is ready. A production-quality
   setup would add a health check plus readiness-aware dependency handling or
   retry model loading with backoff.

7. `mlflow` publishes port 5000 and `frontend` publishes port 8501. `inference`
   has no published port. This matches the Compose file.

8. The running inference process keeps using the model loaded at startup. After
   moving the `champion` alias to the new version, `docker compose restart
   inference` reloads the model selected by that alias. (`Staging` in the lab
   handout is the older MLflow stage terminology.)

9. Restarting creates a new API process from the existing image. Code and
   dependencies are baked into the image, while the model is fetched from MLflow
   during application startup, so changing only the registered model does not
   require rebuilding the image.

10. A normal `docker compose down` removes containers and the network but keeps
    the named volume, so registrations, aliases, and artifacts survive. Adding
    `-v` deletes the named volume too; the next `up` creates an empty MLflow
    database and artifact store.

11. Compose is a single-host orchestrator. Multiple inference replicas need an
    orchestrator and load balancer/service discovery (for example Kubernetes or
    Docker Swarm). MLflow also needs an external highly available database and
    shared object storage, plus scheduling/failover across multiple machines.
